---
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
import CtaBand from "~/components/CtaBand.astro";
import { site } from "~/data/site";
---
<Base
  title="About MyWebMaster — the studio behind Autopilot"
  description="MyWebMaster is a UK web engineering studio. We built Autopilot because website maintenance keeps sites alive without ever making them any better."
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "About", href: "/about/" }]}
>
  <section class="section head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="label">About</p>
      <h1>We got tired of invoicing for maintenance that maintained nothing.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        {site.name} builds and looks after websites for small businesses in the UK. We have been
        doing it for years, and the same uncomfortable pattern kept repeating.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap wrap--narrow prose">
      <h2>The problem we kept watching</h2>
      <p>
        A client pays for a site. It launches, and on that day it is the best it will ever be.
        Then a maintenance plan starts: updates get applied, backups run, uptime is monitored,
        and the invoices go out monthly. Three years later the site is perfectly maintained and
        performing worse than the day it launched, because nothing in that list was ever aimed
        at making it better.
      </p>
      <p>
        Meanwhile the work that would have helped — the broken link nobody noticed, the page
        title that has been costing clicks since 2021, the query the site is shown for four
        hundred times a month and answers nowhere — sits in a document from a paid audit that
        nobody had time to action.
      </p>
      <p>
        The problem was never working out what to do. It was the gap between knowing and
        actually getting round to it — and closing that gap is exactly what software is good at.
      </p>

      <h2>Why we run it on ourselves first</h2>
      <p>
        We thought about launching this with a folder of client results. We could not do it
        honestly. Real results take a month to exist, and anything we showed sooner would have
        been random movement dressed up as evidence.
      </p>
      <p>
        So this domain is the test subject. The baseline was published before any work started —
        {" "}
        <a href="/proof/">shown in Google 18,001 times in 90 days, for 26 visits</a> — and every
        change since is <a href="/changelog/">listed publicly</a>, including the ones that fail.
        If this does not work, this website is where you will see it first.
      </p>

      <h2>What we believe</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>A number with nothing to compare it against is just a story.</strong> Visitor
          numbers move for a hundred reasons. If you cannot separate your own work from the
          weather, you are not measuring anything — you are narrating.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Anything automatic needs published limits.</strong> Software with unlimited
          freedom to change a business's website is a liability, however clever it is. Ours has
          limits, written as a <a href="/what-it-changes/">table anyone can read</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The mistakes should be as visible as the wins.</strong> A list of changes with
          nothing but successes on it tells you the list has been tidied, not that the work is good.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>You should be able to walk away.</strong> The site and everything we improve
          on it are yours the whole time, and we keep a copy of how it was before we started in
          case you want that back instead. Trapping customers is what you do when you are not
          worth keeping.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Where we are</h2>
      <p>
        We work remotely with businesses across the United Kingdom. If you want to know whether
        this suits your site, the <a href="/contact/">quickest way is a free review</a>: we go
        through the whole thing once and send you the list of what we would fix, whether or not
        you ever buy anything.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand />
</Base>

<style>
  .head h1 { margin-block: 1rem 1.2rem; max-width: 20ch; }
  .prose h2 { margin-top: 2.5em; }
  .prose ul { padding-left: 1.2rem; }
  .prose li + li { margin-top: 0.9rem; }
  .prose a { color: var(--accent); }
</style>
